{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 6, :comments ["<b>From the sole. Of soundness.</b> A place in which there is wholeness, like health, and it is anomalous in grammar, for it ought to have been on the pattern of מָעוֹז (Isaiah 25:4), and like it: אֵין מְתֹם בְּשָׂרִי (Psalms 38:4). Rabbi Moses the Kohen, of blessed memory, says that it is like מֵתִים, because he found מֵעִיר מֵתִים (Judges 20:48), and the meaning is: there is not in them what the dead have, and this is a bit far-fetched." "And the word רַק is lacking. <b>There is no soundness in it</b> — only wound and bruise. And like it: there is no good in man that he should eat and drink (Ecclesiastes 2:24)." "<b>Wound.</b> Crushing of the bone." "<b>And bruise.</b> A blow with which moisture gathers." "<b>Fresh.</b> That is not dry, like: כְּמוֹ לְחִי הַחֲמוֹר טְרִיָּה (Judges 15:16), and likewise in the Ishmaelite tongue." "<b>Not pressed.</b> Like יִזֹּר אֶת הַגֵּזָה (ibid. 6:38), for the wound does not heal until the fluid is pressed out." "Also <b>bandaged</b> has the meaning of closing and staunching, and from the same root: לֹא אֶהְיֶה חוֹבֵשׁ (Isaiah 3:7)." "<b>With oil.</b> That it was fitting for it."], :book :isaiah, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 1, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [["Ibn Ezra on Isaiah, by M. Friedlander; Society of Hebrew Literature, London 1877" "https://www.nli.org.il/he/books/NNL_ALEPH001338443"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:51:30.676726548Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}